ef 17592186476278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476772 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "23db8232-d6d6-5122-80a2-143130bc3231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476784 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4036f65d-4a9f-5e2e-aa37-47bc9e7f7c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476832 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a68816de-9c55-5f5d-8b88-343571543dfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476854 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db741232-8680-5d6a-bb33-fb3ba535c7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476918 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of visitors to a museum in an hour.\n\nII. The number obtained when rolling a fair die.\n\nIII. The amount of time, in minutes, between successive full charges in a smartphone." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7feb9bd7-7758-5cb1-b94d-d4d0a23f7141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476918 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of visitors can be any positive integer. Moreover, each number of visitors is not equally likely.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers as possible outcomes, between $1$ and $6,$ and since the die is fair, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the amount of time between events is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476936 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476936 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476936 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The outcome after tossing a fair coin.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $2$ and $5$ at random.\n\nIII. The letter obtained when a letter from the word Mississippi is chosen at random." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476936 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab0e6acf-17b8-5853-9480-fac608a33b73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476936 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of possible outcomes after tossing a coin: Head and tail, and since the coin is fair, each outcome has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $2$ and $5,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because so letters occur more often than others. For example, there is a lower probability of selecting the letter \"M\".\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476943 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476943 #edb/ref 1071 "A particular traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Each day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August has an equal probability of being chosen. What is the probability that the festival will take place before the $18$th of August next year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aef0f15c-e4b9-546e-bc50-4ed011233821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476943 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the date of the festival. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<18).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and the set $S=\\{16,17,…,25\\}$ has $|S|=25-16+1=10$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & x=16,17,…,25 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<18$ are $16,17.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<18)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476976 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when spinning a roulette wheel that is biased towards landing on $0.$\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a rational number between $0$ and $1$ at random.\n\nIII. The IQ of a randomly selected student." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb3352e8-034f-58da-933d-623fe3ee6c62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476976 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $0.$\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many rational numbers between $0$ and $1,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there are a small number of students with very low and very high IQs.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476978 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186476983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476978 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0afaf5eb-a1a3-531d-950b-531438c2a1c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476978 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186476997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b576ed1-e681-535b-add8-5a4e3ec59f62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186476943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476992 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9110cd90-d856-52de-b2a7-c1f02c3ad17c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476992 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186476918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476997 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186477002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476997 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c76af0ff-119c-5482-815e-41a5a57f8cc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476997 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186476976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186477002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186476992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186477002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fcd36363-9a82-5b2b-b02a-0de53a244207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186477002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186476936 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
